WEBVTT

00:00.330 --> 00:04.845
In a group chat, Maya offers to pay for Dev’s train ticket to a reunion.

00:05.254 --> 00:07.168
She means that she wants him there.

00:07.576 --> 00:11.556
Dev hears that he cannot manage his own life, and declines sharply.

00:11.951 --> 00:13.226
Maya feels hurt.

00:13.609 --> 00:17.780
Before explaining either person’s character, ask what accepting the money

00:17.793 --> 00:19.056
would mean to each of them.

00:20.080 --> 00:24.965
A belief is something you think is true, such as expecting a gift to create a debt.

00:25.362 --> 00:30.081
A value is something that matters to you, such as independence or generosity.

00:30.489 --> 00:35.400
Identity includes how you see yourself, perhaps as someone others can rely on.

00:35.808 --> 00:39.800
These are possible concerns in this example, not hidden motives we

00:39.813 --> 00:41.203
can announce as facts.

00:42.247 --> 00:46.226
Dev might want clearer terms about whether the money is a gift or a loan.

00:46.635 --> 00:50.641
He might dislike the public offer, prefer another way to join, or

00:50.653 --> 00:52.337
simply not want the reunion.

00:52.720 --> 00:56.623
A complicated psychological explanation can be less accurate than

00:56.635 --> 00:58.077
an ordinary preference.

00:58.472 --> 01:02.018
Maya can ask privately whether there is something about the offer he

01:02.031 --> 01:03.434
wants her to understand.

01:04.455 --> 01:09.111
Motivated reasoning is reasoning in which what you want to be true influences how

01:09.123 --> 01:10.386
you judge information.

01:10.770 --> 01:13.334
Maya wants to see herself as considerate.

01:13.729 --> 01:17.696
That could make her dismiss Dev’s complaint that the offer was embarrassing.

01:18.091 --> 01:21.969
Psychologist Ziva Kunda examined research on this pattern.

01:22.377 --> 01:27.453
The research helps explain a possibility; it does not decide which friend is right.

01:28.497 --> 01:32.719
Perhaps Dev has experienced gifts that later became obligations.

01:33.103 --> 01:34.455
Perhaps he has not.

01:34.863 --> 01:38.639
One refusal cannot establish a past betrayal or trauma.

01:39.008 --> 01:43.191
People’s accounts of their lives can help explain what matters to them, but they

01:43.204 --> 01:44.747
decide how much to share.

01:45.144 --> 01:49.595
Saying I prefer to keep money separate from friendships gives useful information

01:49.608 --> 01:51.866
without requiring a private life story.

01:52.913 --> 01:57.594
Maya can explain that she wanted Dev at the reunion, acknowledge that a public offer

01:57.607 --> 02:00.528
felt embarrassing, and ask about a different plan.

02:00.925 --> 02:04.483
She does not have to abandon the idea that help can be kind.

02:04.879 --> 02:08.654
Dev can explain that he wants to come but does not want an expensive

02:08.667 --> 02:10.006
trip to become a debt.

02:10.414 --> 02:12.621
Those answers give them new choices.

02:13.663 --> 02:17.885
Suppose they disagree about whether Maya demanded repayment last time.

02:18.294 --> 02:21.445
They can check what was agreed, if a record exists.

02:21.853 --> 02:26.368
If the earlier terms remain unclear, they can make future terms explicit.

02:26.751 --> 02:31.368
If Maya added a condition she had not mentioned, she needs to acknowledge that.

02:31.751 --> 02:36.126
Understanding a person’s meaning does not make disputed facts disappear.

02:37.163 --> 02:41.207
Dev can value independence and still ask for help on another occasion.

02:41.616 --> 02:46.182
A past description is not a contract about how someone must always behave.

02:46.578 --> 02:51.157
Maya does not need to explain his whole personality to make an affordable plan.

02:51.565 --> 02:56.667
And neither person can use that is just who I am to excuse repeated hurtful behaviour.

02:57.705 --> 03:02.386
When admitting a mistake feels uncomfortable, ask: What do I want to be true?

03:02.756 --> 03:06.723
Consider information that deserves a fair hearing, even if it means

03:06.736 --> 03:08.828
apologising or changing a plan.

03:09.237 --> 03:10.895
You may keep your conclusion.

03:11.303 --> 03:15.627
The task is to examine how you reached it, while giving the other person room to

03:15.640 --> 03:17.004
explain their own meaning.
